ains 6 random integers between 0 (inclusive) and 5 (exclusive), call the ","key":"FH5h16VWej"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"set_seed()","key":"USH736EGHd"},{"type":"text","value":" function and use ","key":"gYKfvYo4TO"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"42","key":"HZaJFXNkfb"},{"type":"text","value":" as the seed parameter.","key":"GN78zFywmd"}],"key":"U9HrCsv59K"}],"key":"f6hauwJnRW"},{"type":"break","key":"Rii0S4Cqhs"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Create a tensor “x” from the array above","key":"HA5MuNMlsq"}],"key":"SWDhDvxr67"}],"key":"RT7IeVW4mC"},{"type":"break","key":"CXDbSI3qlt"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5. Change the dtype of x from ","key":"kXaJcBZlm4"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"int32","key":"Rtc2yPn35o"},{"type":"text","value":" to ","key":"q9JIyQzn03"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"int64","key":"uojQbRfSQk"}],"key":"vhMVTSs4i6"}],"key":"W2NBGAi2ic"},{"type":"break","key":"FtC2PX5EiK"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6. Reshape ","key":"ShKFGGaz0V"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"WuZAkOTOWu"},{"type":"text","value":" into a 3x2 tensor","key":"TO2W4mKjt0"}],"key":"GZ0VyAtDnw"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"aPDmDzHGtl"},{"type":"break","key":"woS0rgy6zv"},{"type":"text","value":"There are several ways to do this.","key":"ky7Fxkifri"}],"key":"Fw7jyC0PcW"},{"type":"break","key":"MbBAiKOLtE"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7. Return the right-hand column of tensor ","key":"hiMDQJDK6k"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"s71PtR9vVK"}],"key":"lZeIpI2uQR"}],"key":"n7euwEBIm7"},{"type":"break","key":"Mh6E6Ve8lV"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"8. Without changing x, return a tensor of square values of ","key":"pJahGSzzj3"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"SJ1CsfYsCf"}],"key":"hZiwwnTRST"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"fZyA00xjff"},{"type":"break","key":"j33X8jRLbQ"},{"type":"text","value":"There are several ways to do this.","key":"jTnFd1mQJL"}],"key":"ntA9Kmybdq"},{"type":"break","key":"i5MwTu7NJX"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"9. Create a tensor ","key":"vbmYKuPpIW"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y","key":"mH02UxaKJ6"},{"type":"text","value":" with the same number of elements as ","key":"QbJGrWR5Bs"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"cXZARJhHsb"},{"type":"text","value":", that can be matrix-multiplied with ","key":"hOBLZ2Rk0P"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"WEOBDkV910"}],"key":"hxWZt8yWDH"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"Yu5kOCXzE1"},{"type":"break","key":"D3vUVhJXrI"},{"type":"text","value":"Use PyTorch directly (not NumPy) to create a tensor of random integers between 0 (inclusive) and 5 (exclusive). Use 42 as seed. ","key":"BcqxDvZJ0J"},{"type":"break","key":"xph2KCROy3"},{"type":"text","value":"Think about what shape it should have to permit matrix multiplication.","key":"UJ3rCHvWte"}],"key":"fuVDEtT5SN"},{"type":"break","key":"Mva5CZyx2m"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"10. Find the matrix product of ","key":"DpaZKtNhgL"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"LV6xSksP7n"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","key":"i2Knu9CFxT"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y","key":"EVBlMTbAsP"},{"type":"text","value":".","key":"MFhpqKm6L2"}],"key":"yg4d97p7Rr"}],"key":"Dpp0SaaJBF"}],"key":"K8e14CvLee"}],"key":"WvT2IohaI3"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Imports and Seed Initialization","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"sCpuiY4hZU"}],"identifier":"id-1-imports-and-seed-initialization","label":"1. Imports and Seed Initialization","html_id":"id-1-imports-and-seed-initialization","implicit":true,"key":"K2lNZ7VJf3"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This section sets up the foundational libraries required for tensor operations and mathematical computations. The set_seed() function is defined to ensure reproducible results across both NumPy and PyTorch environments by fixing the random number generators using torch.manual_seed(). This guarantees that “random” tensor generation will yield the exact same values every time the code runs.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"AbVRIr7tZc"}],"key":"byhCIkogm5"}],"key":"yccR8YVnWv"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 1. Perform Standard Imports\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\n\n# 2. Create a function called set_seed()\ndef set_seed(seed: int):\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    torch.manual_seed(seed)","key":"ttEDmlYDAl"},{"type":"outputs","id":"Uv-Xd5VB-eCeCnSwA1uGx","children":[],"key":"DLp1VoeHGs"}],"key":"zdtJ7GBNmk"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. Array Generation, Tensor Conversion, and Reshaping","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"LvqDzZCUgJ"}],"identifier":"id-2-array-generation-tensor-conversion-and-reshaping","label":"2. Array Generation, Tensor Conversion, and Reshaping","html_id":"id-2-array-generation-tensor-conversion-and-reshaping","implicit":true,"key":"DMotwoxCbA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here, a NumPy array of 6 random integers is generated and immediately converted into a PyTorch tensor using torch.from_numpy(). Because PyTorch operations strictly require matching datatypes, the .type() method is explicitly used to cast the tensor from int32 to a 64-bit integer (torch.int64). Finally, the flat tensor is transformed into a 2D matrix (3 rows and 2 columns) using .reshape(), which safely alters the tensor’s view without modifying the original memory block.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"VDIbwV6Dk0"}],"key":"GfLQBhCsJs"}],"key":"zf639XgXa1"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 3. Create a NumPy array \nset_seed(42)\narr = np.random.randint(0, 5, 6)\n\n# 4. Create a tensor \"x\" from the array above\nx = torch.from_numpy(arr)\n\n# 5. Change the dtype of x from int32 to int64\nx = x.type(torch.int64)\n\n# 6. Reshape x into a 3x2 tensor\nx = x.reshape(3, 2)","key":"koVxcASLUd"},{"type":"outputs","id":"2cV1kIY77LDkr8t4gmGG-","children":[],"key":"R6vngnm4zD"}],"key":"VbgY5TyooZ"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3. Tensor Slicing and Element-wise Operations","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"IJqSJf8RHd"}],"identifier":"id-3-tensor-slicing-and-element-wise-operations","label":"3. Tensor Slicing and Element-wise Operations","html_id":"id-3-tensor-slicing-and-element-wise-operations","implicit":true,"key":"hLBDeG33mm"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This block demonstrates PyTorch’s native data manipulation capabilities. Extracting specific values from a tensor uses the exact same slicing syntax as NumPy arrays. The slice [:, 1:] grabs all rows (:) but only the elements from index 1 onwards (1:), effectively returning the right-hand column as a 2D slice. The .pow(2) function performs an element-wise arithmetic power operation, returning a brand new tensor containing the squared values without altering the original tensor x.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"KtB7HoK6Es"}],"key":"TlCOYYMpsB"}],"key":"IlpX8X8hyy"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 7. Return the right-hand column of tensor x\nright_column = x[:, 1:] \n\n# 8. Without changing x, return a tensor of square values of x\nx_squared = x.pow(2)","key":"mpqCjq8qKY"},{"type":"outputs","id":"nIxK1Fr8RF_rWHTobTANp","children":[],"key":"xKbpmiSWNf"}],"key":"pumJ2iGBIo"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Matrix Creation and Multiplication","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"JKo7GYWOsw"}],"identifier":"id-4-matrix-creation-and-multiplication","label":"4. Matrix Creation and Multiplication","html_id":"id-4-matrix-creation-and-multiplication","implicit":true,"key":"KhN0P2qdpa"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To perform a 2D matrix multiplication, the number of columns in the first tensor must perfectly match the number of rows in the second tensor. Since tensor x has the shape (3, 2), the new tensor y is explicitly created from scratch using torch.randint() with an inverted shape of (2, 3). The operation ","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"fvAki2q62H"},{"type":"link","url":"http://torch.mm","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"torch.mm","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"iVyhWYRXBR"}],"urlSource":"http://torch.mm","key":"W5PXhaGssi"},{"type":"text","value":"(x, y) then calculates the matrix product.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"q7793vAP3B"}],"key":"LQT0gKlD1I"}],"key":"iXES97c3RY"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 9. Create a tensor y with the same number of elements as x, that can be matrix-multiplied with x\nset_seed(42)\ny = torch.randint(0, 5, (2, 3))\n\n# 10. Find the matrix product of x and y\nmatrix_prod = torch.mm(x, y) \n\n# Print statements to verify the outputs\nprint(\"x:\\n\", x)\nprint(\"\\nright_column:\\n\", right_column)\nprint(\"\\nx_squared:\\n\", x_squared)\nprint(\"\\ny:\\n\", y)\nprint(\"\\nMatrix Product (x * y):\\n\", matrix_prod)","key":"MLki5Mg1z3"},{"type":"outputs","id":"oIGIMwYONU0vDXVS2sQFP","children":[{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"x:\n tensor([[3, 4],\n        [2, 4],\n        [4, 1]])\n\nright_column:\n tensor([[4],\n        [4],\n        [1]])\n\nx_squared:\n tensor([[ 9, 16],\n        [ 4, 16],\n        [16,  1]])\n\ny:\n tensor([[2, 2, 1],\n        [4, 1, 0]])\n\nMatrix Product (x * y):\n tensor([[22, 10,  3],\n        [20,  8,  2],\n        [12,  9,  4]])\n"},"children":[],"key":"zPtS0KWCLm"}],"key":"pSaAZtJRrq"}],"key":"TKyGN74sSC"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tensor Reshaping and Slicing Verification","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"pIrlQQ4vWD"}],"key":"Rib0qwfmDu"}],"key":"b49EdLAOa4"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The printed output of x confirms it was successfully reshaped into a 3x2 configuration. The right_column output [[4], [4], [1]] proves that the slicing operation [:, 1:] successfully extracted the final column while maintaining its proper 2D column-vector shape rather than flattening it into a 1D array.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"GY4oTvgdMp"}],"key":"mpgrDNYVC0"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Element-Wise Power Dynamics","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"f3VXthvC4D"}],"key":"BJqsintCnb"},{"type":"text","value":"\nThe x_squared output directly mirrors the shape of x, proving that basic arithmetic operations like pow() are applied on an element-by-element basis. Every individual scalar inside the matrix was multiplied by itself (e.g., the 3 became 9, the 4 became 16), entirely independently of the other elements in the tensor.   Dimensionality and the Matrix Product\nThe final output perfectly demonstrates the mathematical laws of matrix multiplication. Tensor x has a size of (3, 2) and tensor y has a size of (2, 3). Because their inner dimensions match (2 columns and 2 rows), the multiplication is valid. The resulting tensor natively adopts the outer dimensions of the two parent matrices, successfully outputting a new tensor with the expected shape of (3, 3).","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"aWKzj93Sfh"}],"key":"Mttty5kj4y"}],"key":"Cf91vHQ7ol"}],"key":"WCEvyiKnBB"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Laboratory Task 4 (01_Understanding Deep Learning)","short_title":"Task 4: Understanding Deep Learning","url":"/laboratory-task-4-01-understanding-deep-learning","group":"Personal Portfolio"},"next":{"title":"Laboratory   Task  6 ( E1    CNN  Implementation )","short_title":"Task 6: CNN Implementation","url":"/laboratory-task-6-e1-cnn-implementation","group":"Personal Portfolio"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}